  "articleBody": "Installing a new sink and faucet updates your entire kitchen, but small oversights during planning can lead to leaks, wasted money, or countertop damage. Here are the most common pitfalls to steer clear of when updating your fixtures.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n- Skipping the hole-count check: Buying a single-stem faucet without checking whether your existing countertop has three holes leaves exposed openings unless you have a matching deck plate ready.\n- Mismatching spout height and sink depth: A high-arc commercial-style faucet paired with a shallow 6-inch basin often creates excessive splash-back onto counters and drywall.\n- Reusing old, stiff supply lines: Braided lines and rubber washers degrade over time. Reusing twenty-year-old supply lines to save a few dollars frequently causes slow, hidden leaks inside the cabinet base.\n- Overtightening plumbing nuts: Cranking down too hard on plastic sink nuts or brass compression fittings cracks gaskets and strips threads, creating immediate leaks.\n- Forgetting to flush the lines before use: Construction debris and mineral sediment dislodged during installation can clog new faucet cartridges and aerators if lines are not flushed before testing.\n\nWhat to Do Next\nIf your project involves corroded drain pipes, an undermount stone installation, or shutoff valves that will not budge, professional help prevents messy water leaks. \n\nContact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for quality kitchen fixture installation across Denver and surrounding communities.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}